  "generated_text": "To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( -90 \\le n \\le 90 \\) and \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 419^\\circ\\), we need to use the periodic and symmetric properties of the sine function. Specifically, we use the fact that \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(\\theta + 360^\\circ k)\\) for any integer \\( k \\), and the identity \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(180^\\circ - \\theta)\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\( 419^\\circ \\) to an equivalent angle between \\( 0^\\circ \\) and \\( 360^\\circ \\):\n\\[ 419^\\circ = 420^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 420^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 59^\\circ. \\]\nSo, \\(\\sin 419^\\circ = \\sin 59^\\circ\\).\n\nNext, we need to find an angle \\( n \\) such that \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 59^\\circ\\) and \\( -90 \\le n \\le 90 \\). Since \\( \\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(180^\\circ - \\theta) \\), we have:\n\\[ \\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 59^\\circ) = \\sin 121^\\circ. \\]\nHowever, \\( 121^\\circ \\) is not in the range \\( -90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ \\). Instead, we use the fact that \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(-\\theta)\\) and the range constraint to find the equivalent angle in the desired range. The angle \\( 121^\\circ \\) is in the second quadrant, and the reference angle is \\( 180^\\circ - 121^\\circ = 59^\\circ \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ \\sin 121^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 121^\\circ) = \\sin 59^\\circ. \\]\nSince \\( 121^\\circ \\) is not in the range \\( -90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ \\), we use the negative angle equivalent:\n\\[ \\sin 121^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 121^\\circ) = \\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin (-121^\\circ). \\]\nBut we need the angle in the range \\( -90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ \\), so we take:\n\\[ n = -121^\\circ. \\]\nHowever, \\(-121^\\circ\\) is not in the range \\(-90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ\\). Instead, we use the positive equivalent:\n\\[ n = 180^\\circ - 121^\\circ = 59^\\circ. \\]\nSo, the correct angle in the range \\(-90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ\\) is:\n\\[ n = 59^\\circ. \\]\n\nThus, the integer \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{59}\\).",
